      "narrationText": "Acorns!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n23.9 grams of fat. Saturated fat is 3.1 grams, supporting a better fat profile. Omega 3 is 40 milligrams, and for nuts, fat quality has to justify the calories.\n-\n40.8 grams of carbs. Sugar is 0 grams, supporting better carb quality.\n-\n6.2 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 3 out of 9, with 55% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin E is 10% daily value, adding useful vitamin support.\n-\nMagnesium is 18% daily value, adding useful mineral support. Iron is only 4% daily value, a weak mark for nuts.\n-\nThe upsides first: holds up as a better snack default than most packaged options. That real-world swap value counts. Fat and texture help satiety. They can hold up better than many snack foods. Portable, practical snack food. Nuts score real points in life because they are easy to use.\n-\nCons next: cost or allergy issues can limit use. Practical downsides still count. Good versions can get expensive. That affects how realistic frequent use is. Calorie density is very high. That makes portion control a real issue.\n-\nBig strengths are protein and fibre, but the biggest weaknesses are cost or allergy issues can limit use and good versions can get expensive.\n-\nC tier.\n"
