      "narrationText": "Pear!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n0.1 grams of fat. Saturated fat is 0 grams, supporting a better fat profile. Polyunsaturated fat is 0.1 grams, a weak mark for fruits.\n-\n15.2 grams of carbs. Glycemic index is 38 GI, supporting better carb quality. Starch is 0 grams, a meaningful miss for fruits.\n-\n0.4 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 1 out of 9, with 28% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin C is 5% daily value, useful for collagen formation and antioxidant support. Vitamin A is only 0% daily value, a weak mark for fruits.\n-\nPotassium is 3% daily value, useful for fluid balance. Iron is only 1% daily value, a weak mark for fruits.\n-\nPositives first: usually beats ultra-processed sweet snacks. That replacement value matters in real life. Easy to use as a whole food. Simple, recognisable, and realistic for everyday eating. Ingredient simplicity is hard to argue with. Whole-fruit clarity is part of the category appeal.\n-\nDownsides next: easy to eat quickly when it is very sweet. That can blur the line between light and sugar-heavy portions. Satiety is not especially strong. Fruit often needs help from the rest of the meal. Protein contribution is tiny. That is normal for fruit, but it still limits nutritional breadth.\n-\nBig strengths are omega 3 and fibre, but the biggest weaknesses are easy to eat quickly when it is very sweet and satiety is not especially strong.\n-\nC tier.\n"
