      "narrationText": "Asparagus!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n0.1 grams of fat. Omega 3 is 40 milligrams, supporting a more useful fat profile. Polyunsaturated fat is 0.1 grams, a weak mark for vegetables.\n-\n3.9 grams of carbs. Glycemic index is 15 GI, supporting better carb quality. Starch is 0 grams, a small miss for vegetables.\n-\n2.2 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 3 out of 9, with 40% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin K is 35% daily value, adding useful vitamin support. Vitamin C is 8% daily value, a weak mark for vegetables.\n-\nIron is 12% daily value, useful for oxygen transport. Calcium is only 2% daily value, a weak mark for vegetables.\n-\nPros first: makes meals feel more balanced. The category has real value beyond single-nutrient stats. Very low downside whole food. Vegetables win a lot simply by offering clean nutritional payoff. Good volume for the calories. That matters for satiety and general usefulness.\n-\nDownsides next: energy density is very low. Good for some use cases, but not enough on its own. Usually needs pairing to make a full meal. Most vegetables are not complete by themselves. Freshness and prep can matter. Practical friction is still a real downside.\n-\nBig strengths are omega 3 and carb quality, but the biggest weaknesses are energy density is very low and usually needs pairing to make a full meal.\n-\nB tier.\n"
