      "narrationText": "Bulgur!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n1.3 grams of fat. Saturated fat is 0.2 grams, supporting a better fat profile. Omega 3 is 40 milligrams, a weak mark for grains.\n-\n75.9 grams of carbs. Fibre is 12.5 grams, helping with digestion and steadier meals.\n-\n12.3 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 4 out of 9, with 58% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin E is 0% daily value, adding useful vitamin support.\n-\nMagnesium is 41% daily value, adding useful mineral support. Potassium is 12% daily value, a weak mark for grains.\n-\nThe upsides first: usually affordable and scalable. Practicality matters for staple foods. Useful staple energy source. That matters because grains are often judged by how practical they are in real diets. Works as a meal anchor. A lot of value comes from being easy to build around.\n-\nThe drawbacks next: quality can drop fast with processing. The grain category punishes refinement pretty hard. Easy to overshoot portions. Staple carbs can pile up fast without much friction. Carb load is still the main burden. That is the category risk no grain fully escapes.\n-\nBig strengths are protein and fibre, but the biggest weaknesses are quality can drop fast with processing and easy to overshoot portions.\n-\nB tier.\n"
