      "narrationText": "Butter!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n81.1 grams of fat. Saturated fat is 51.4 grams, supporting a better fat profile.\n-\n0.1 grams of carbs. No defensible carb submacros to call out. Not relevant here.\n-\n0.9 grams of protein. No defensible protein submacros to call out. Also not relevant here.\n-\nVitamin A is 28% daily value, useful for vision and immune support. Vitamin K is 6% daily value, a weak mark for oils and fats.\n-\nCalcium is 2% daily value, adding useful mineral support. Iron is only 0% daily value, a weak mark for oils and fats.\n-\nPros first: simple traditional fat. This gives butter a cleaner identity than heavily processed fat substitutes. Very strong culinary usefulness. Butter has real kitchen legitimacy and practical use that matters in this category. Can fit as a moderate traditional fat. The editorial position here is that butter belongs as a flawed but legitimate middle-ground fat rather than a bottom-tier industrial product.\n-\nCons next: saturated fat is a major drawback. This is the main reason butter cannot be an elite oils-and-fats food. Cholesterol is high. A second meaningful strike against the fat profile. Fat profile is clearly weaker than top-tier oils. Butter is not competing with the best oils on chemistry alone.\n-\nBig strengths are simple traditional fat and strong culinary usefulness, but the biggest weaknesses are saturated fat and cholesterol is high.\n-\nC tier.\n"
