      "narrationText": "Flaxseed Oil!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n100 grams of fat. Saturated fat is 9 grams, supporting a better fat profile.\n-\n0 grams of carbs. No defensible carb submacros to call out. Not relevant here.\n-\n0 grams of protein. No defensible protein submacros to call out. Also not relevant here.\n-\nVitamin K is 10% daily value, adding useful vitamin support. Vitamin A is only 0% daily value, a weak mark for oils and fats.\n-\nIron is 0% daily value, useful for oxygen transport. Magnesium is only 0% daily value, a weak mark for oils and fats.\n-\nPros first: highly useful in cooking. Practical culinary value matters here. Can help meals feel more complete and usable. Fats sometimes improve the whole plate, not just their own stats. Small amounts can change a whole meal. That leverage matters when the fat quality is good.\n-\nDownsides next: not the most forgiving cooking fat. Great chemistry on paper, but use case is narrower than the headline suggests. Small serving errors matter quickly. A little extra oil or fat can move the calorie math fast. Calorie density is brutally high. Tiny serving errors matter here.\n-\nBig strengths are fat quality and useful in cooking, but the biggest weaknesses are not the most forgiving cooking fat and small serving errors matter quickly.\n-\nB tier.\n"
