      "narrationText": "Ice Cream!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n11 grams of fat. Cholesterol is 44 milligrams, supporting a better fat profile.\n-\n24 grams of carbs. Glycemic index is 61 GI, supporting better carb quality. Starch is 0 grams, a small miss for dairy.\n-\n3.5 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 4 out of 9, with 70% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin A is 8% daily value, useful for vision and immune support. Vitamin B12 is 6% daily value, a weak mark for dairy.\n-\nCalcium is 13% daily value, adding useful mineral support. Magnesium is only 2% daily value, a weak mark for dairy.\n-\nPositives first: high enjoyment factor. A real-world reason people eat it, even if the category fit is weak. Still retains some calcium. Not nutritionally empty, just weak compared with stronger dairy foods. Still clearly dairy-based. That matters for fairness when picking anchors.\n-\nDownsides next: sugar load is a major dairy-category problem. This pushes it away from what strong dairy foods are supposed to deliver. Saturated fat is a major drawback. A second major strike against the food in this category. Protein usefulness is weak for dairy. That makes it a poor fit compared with stronger dairy anchors like yogurt.\n-\nBig strengths are protein and high enjoyment factor, but the biggest weaknesses are sugar load is a major dairy-category problem and saturated fat.\n-\nD tier.\n"
