      "narrationText": "Plain Kefir!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n3.5 grams of fat. Cholesterol is 13 milligrams, supporting a better fat profile. Polyunsaturated fat is 0.1 grams, a weak mark for dairy.\n-\n4.8 grams of carbs. Sugar is 4.8 grams, supporting better carb quality. Starch is 0 grams, a small miss for dairy.\n-\n3.5 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 7 out of 9, with 90% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance. Collagen is only 0 grams, a weak mark for dairy.\n-\nVitamin B12 is 12% daily value, useful for nerve and blood-cell support. Vitamin A is only 4% daily value, a weak mark for dairy.\n-\nCalcium is 10% daily value, adding useful mineral support. Magnesium is only 3% daily value, a weak mark for dairy.\n-\nPros first: fermented dairy practicality is a real plus. It keeps the category strengths while usually being a bit more interesting in practice. Clear calcium identity. That is a core category strength. Easy protein delivery for normal diets. Dairy gets real credit for convenience.\n-\nDownsides next: processing changes the category a lot. The label alone does not tell the whole story. Some versions get dense fast on fat, sodium, or both. The category swings a lot by format. Not everyone tolerates dairy well. That is a real use-case limitation.\n-\nBig strengths are omega 3 and protein, but the biggest weaknesses are processing changes the category a lot and some versions get dense fast on fat, sodium, or both.\n-\nB tier.\n"
