      "narrationText": "Lima Beans!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n0.7 grams of fat. Saturated fat is 0.2 grams, supporting a better fat profile.\n-\n63.4 grams of carbs. Fibre is 19 grams, helping with digestion and steadier meals.\n-\n21.5 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 6 out of 9, with 72% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin C is 2% daily value, useful for collagen formation and antioxidant support. Vitamin E is only 1% daily value, a weak mark for legumes.\n-\nMagnesium is 32% daily value, adding useful mineral support. Zinc is 18% daily value, a weak mark for legumes.\n-\nPositives first: filling for the calories. Legumes usually earn a lot of practical value from satiety. Does a lot of jobs at once. Protein, fibre, and meal utility are why legumes stay valuable. Fibre-forward category identity. Legumes are supposed to bring stable carbs and fibre.\n-\nDownsides next: protein quality still has a ceiling. Good, but not meat or dairy level. Can take planning or prep. That is a small but real friction cost. Prep time can still be a barrier. Dry legumes especially ask more from the cook.\n-\nBig strengths are protein and fibre, but the biggest weaknesses are protein quality still has a ceiling and can take planning or prep.\n-\nA tier.\n"
