      "narrationText": "Onion!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n0.1 grams of fat. Omega 3 is 10 milligrams, supporting a more useful fat profile. Polyunsaturated fat is 0 grams, a weak mark for vegetables.\n-\n9.3 grams of carbs. Glycemic index is 10 GI, supporting better carb quality. Starch is 0 grams, a small miss for vegetables.\n-\n1.1 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 1 out of 9, with 35% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin C is 8% daily value, useful for collagen formation and antioxidant support. Vitamin A is only 0% daily value, a weak mark for vegetables.\n-\nPotassium is 3% daily value, useful for fluid balance. Iron is only 1% daily value, a weak mark for vegetables.\n-\nPros first: makes meals feel more balanced. The category has real value beyond single-nutrient stats. Helps add volume without much calorie cost. That makes vegetables easier to use often. Good volume for the calories. That matters for satiety and general usefulness.\n-\nThe drawbacks next: energy density is very low. Good for some use cases, but not enough on its own. Taste and texture can limit compliance. A strong paper profile still needs real-world repeatability. Rarely carries a meal by itself. That is normal here, but still part of the tradeoff.\n-\nBig strengths are omega 3 and makes meals feel more balanced, but the biggest weaknesses are energy density is very low and taste and texture can limit compliance.\n-\nD tier.\n"
