      "narrationText": "Pecans!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n72 grams of fat. Omega 3 is 1000 milligrams, supporting a more useful fat profile.\n-\n13.9 grams of carbs. Glycemic index is 10 GI, supporting better carb quality. Starch is 0 grams, a small miss for nuts.\n-\n9.2 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 4 out of 9, with 68% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin E is 10% daily value, adding useful vitamin support.\n-\nMagnesium is 34% daily value, adding useful mineral support. Iron is 14% daily value, a weak mark for nuts.\n-\nPositives first: fat and texture help satiety. They can hold up better than many snack foods. Holds up as a better snack default than most packaged options. That real-world swap value counts. Texture helps them feel satisfying. That can make moderation easier than with softer snack foods.\n-\nDownsides next: cost or allergy issues can limit use. Practical downsides still count. Good versions can get expensive. That affects how realistic frequent use is. Portion control can get slippery fast. Dense foods punish casual handful math.\n-\nBig strengths are omega 3 and protein, but the biggest weaknesses are cost or allergy issues can limit use and good versions can get expensive.\n-\nC tier.\n"
