      "narrationText": "Sesame Seeds!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n49.7 grams of fat. Polyunsaturated fat is 21.8 grams, helping with cell structure and healthy signalling.\n-\n23.4 grams of carbs. Fibre is 11.8 grams, helping with digestion and steadier meals. Starch is 0.3 grams, a small miss for seeds.\n-\n17.7 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 6 out of 9, with 56% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin E is 2% daily value, adding useful vitamin support. Vitamin B12 is only 0% daily value, a weak mark for seeds.\n-\nMagnesium is 88% daily value, adding useful mineral support. Zinc is 70% daily value, a weak mark for seeds.\n-\nPositives first: elite mineral density. The mineral payload is a major category strength beyond a normal seed baseline. Easy to use across lots of foods. Works well in savoury dishes, baking, and toppings. Strong wholefood seed identity. Feels like a very legitimate seed-category food rather than an edge case.\n-\nCons next: contains some anti-nutrients. Phytates may reduce mineral uptake somewhat. Not suitable for everyone. Sesame allergy risk matters in practice. Saturated fat is not negligible. Still not a disaster, but it is less clean than the best fat-profile seeds.\n-\nBig strengths are omega 3 and protein, but the biggest weaknesses are contains some anti-nutrients and not suitable for everyone.\n-\nB tier.\n"
