      "narrationText": "White Potato!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n0.1 grams of fat. Saturated fat is 0 grams, supporting a better fat profile. Polyunsaturated fat is 0 grams, a weak mark for tubers.\n-\n17.5 grams of carbs. Glycemic index is 59 GI, supporting better carb quality.\n-\n2 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 2 out of 9, with 35% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin C is 22% daily value, useful for collagen formation and antioxidant support. Vitamin A is only 0% daily value, a weak mark for tubers.\n-\nPotassium is 9% daily value, useful for fluid balance. Iron is only 4% daily value, a weak mark for tubers.\n-\nPositives first: very practical staple food. Cheap, common, and easy to build meals around, which matters for a tuber category built on energy foods. Can be fairly filling. Especially when eaten in less processed forms. Brings some useful potassium. Not amazing, but enough to matter a bit in the category.\n-\nCons next: glycemic behaviour is only middling. Not as clean as the best tubers on the carb-behaviour axis. Fibre is only modest. Decent, but not category-leading. Micronutrient return is fine rather than elite. Strong enough to be respectable, not enough to be exceptional.\n-\nBig strengths are omega 3 and protein, but the biggest weaknesses are glycemic behaviour is only middling and fibre is only modest.\n-\nS tier.\n"
