      "narrationText": "Zucchini!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n0.3 grams of fat. Omega 3 is 15 milligrams, supporting a more useful fat profile. Polyunsaturated fat is 0.1 grams, a weak mark for vegetables.\n-\n3.1 grams of carbs. Glycemic index is 15 GI, supporting better carb quality. Starch is 0 grams, a small miss for vegetables.\n-\n1.2 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 2 out of 9, with 35% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin C is 20% daily value, useful for collagen formation and antioxidant support. Vitamin A is only 4% daily value, a weak mark for vegetables.\n-\nPotassium is 6% daily value, useful for fluid balance. Calcium is only 1% daily value, a weak mark for vegetables.\n-\nPros first: good volume for the calories. That matters for satiety and general usefulness. Helps add volume without much calorie cost. That makes vegetables easier to use often. Easy to build meals around. A practical plus because good foods need realistic use cases.\n-\nCons next: rarely carries a meal by itself. That is normal here, but still part of the tradeoff. Usually needs pairing to make a full meal. Most vegetables are not complete by themselves. Energy density is very low. Good for some use cases, but not enough on its own.\n-\nBig strengths are omega 3 and carb quality, but the biggest weaknesses are rarely carries a meal by itself and usually needs pairing to make a full meal.\n-\nC tier.\n"
