        "narrationText": "Brazil Nuts!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n67.1 grams of fat. Polyunsaturated fat is 24.4 grams, helping with cell structure and healthy signalling. Omega 3 is 30 milligrams, and for nuts, fat quality has to justify the calories.\n-\n11.7 grams of carbs. Glycemic index is 10 GI, supporting better carb quality. Starch is 0 grams, a small miss for nuts.\n-\n14.3 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 5 out of 9, with 72% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin E is 38% daily value, adding useful vitamin support.\n-\nMagnesium is 89% daily value, adding useful mineral support. Iron is 14% daily value, a weak mark for nuts.\n-\nThe upsides first: texture helps them feel satisfying. That can make moderation easier than with softer snack foods. Portable, practical snack food. Nuts score real points in life because they are easy to use. Fat and texture help satiety. They can hold up better than many snack foods.\n-\nThe drawbacks next: easy to overdo in real portions. Tiny serving sizes can make the practical use case awkward. Cost or allergy issues can limit use. Practical downsides still count. Calorie density is very high. That makes portion control a real issue.\n-\nBig strengths are protein and fibre, but the biggest weaknesses are easy to overdo in real portions and cost or allergy issues can limit use.\n-\nA tier.\n"
