        "narrationText": "Buckwheat!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n3.4 grams of fat. Saturated fat is 0.7 grams, supporting a better fat profile. Omega 3 is 80 milligrams, a weak mark for grains.\n-\n71.5 grams of carbs. Fibre is 10 grams, helping with digestion and steadier meals.\n-\n13.3 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 5 out of 9, with 60% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin E is 1% daily value, adding useful vitamin support.\n-\nMagnesium is 55% daily value, adding useful mineral support. Potassium is 13% daily value, a weak mark for grains.\n-\nPositives first: useful staple energy source. That matters because grains are often judged by how practical they are in real diets. Easy to batch cook at scale. That practicality is part of why grains matter. Works as a meal anchor. A lot of value comes from being easy to build around.\n-\nCons next: quality can drop fast with processing. The grain category punishes refinement pretty hard. Needs strong pairings to feel complete. The grain rarely finishes the nutrition job alone. Protein quality is limited. Even the better grains usually need pairing.\n-\nBig strengths are protein and fibre, but the biggest weaknesses are quality can drop fast with processing and needs strong pairings to feel complete.\n-\nB tier.\n"
