        "narrationText": "Coconut Oil!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n100 grams of fat. Cholesterol is 0 milligrams, supporting a better fat profile. Saturated fat is 82.5 grams, and for oils and fats, that matters a lot.\n-\n0 grams of carbs. No defensible carb submacros to call out. Not relevant here.\n-\n0 grams of protein. No defensible protein submacros to call out. Also not relevant here.\n-\nVitamin E is 1% daily value, adding useful vitamin support. Vitamin A is only 0% daily value, a weak mark for oils and fats.\n-\nIron is 0% daily value, useful for oxygen transport. Magnesium is only 0% daily value, a weak mark for oils and fats.\n-\nThe upsides first: strong cooking-stability case. Tests whether practical cooking arguments can rescue brutal fat chemistry. Simple ingredient identity. Cleaner than industrial spreads. Strong healthy-food halo. Likely audience argument magnet.\n-\nDownsides next: saturated fat is extreme. This should hammer it hard if the category means what it says. Overall fat profile is weak. Very poor fit for an elite fats ranking. Health reputation can outrun the data. Useful editorial counterweight.\n-\nBig strengths are strong cooking-stability case and simple ingredient identity, but the biggest weaknesses are saturated fat is extreme and overall fat profile is weak.\n-\nD tier.\n"
