        "narrationText": "Cornmeal!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n3.9 grams of fat. Saturated fat is 0.5 grams, supporting a better fat profile. Omega 3 is 40 milligrams, a weak mark for grains.\n-\n79 grams of carbs. Fibre is 7.3 grams, helping with digestion and steadier meals. Glycemic index is 68 GI, a meaningful miss for grains.\n-\n7.1 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 3 out of 9, with 50% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin E is 1% daily value, adding useful vitamin support.\n-\nMagnesium is 18% daily value, adding useful mineral support. Potassium is only 4% daily value, a weak mark for grains.\n-\nPositives first: can keep meals cheap and steady. That is a genuine category strength. Works as a meal anchor. A lot of value comes from being easy to build around. Usually affordable and scalable. Practicality matters for staple foods.\n-\nCons next: needs strong pairings to feel complete. The grain rarely finishes the nutrition job alone. Protein quality is limited. Even the better grains usually need pairing. Carb load is still the main burden. That is the category risk no grain fully escapes.\n-\nBig strengths are omega 3 and protein, but the biggest weaknesses are needs strong pairings to feel complete and protein quality is limited.\n-\nC tier.\n"
