        "narrationText": "Duck Fat!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n100 grams of fat. Saturated fat is 33 grams, supporting a better fat profile.\n-\n0 grams of carbs. No defensible carb submacros to call out. Not relevant here.\n-\n0 grams of protein. No defensible protein submacros to call out. Also not relevant here.\n-\nVitamin K is 6% daily value, adding useful vitamin support. Vitamin A is only 0% daily value, a weak mark for oils and fats.\n-\nIron is 0% daily value, useful for oxygen transport. Magnesium is only 0% daily value, a weak mark for oils and fats.\n-\nThe upsides first: highly useful in cooking. Practical culinary value matters here. Efficient energy source. That can be a strength in the right context. Can help meals feel more complete and usable. Fats sometimes improve the whole plate, not just their own stats.\n-\nThe drawbacks next: extremely calorie dense by default. That is the unavoidable category burden. Small serving errors matter quickly. A little extra oil or fat can move the calorie math fast. Easy to overpour without noticing. Practical portion creep is real.\n-\nBig strengths are useful in cooking and efficient energy source, but the biggest weaknesses are extremely calorie dense by default and small serving errors matter quickly.\n-\nC tier.\n"
