        "narrationText": "Kiwi!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n0.5 grams of fat. Saturated fat is 0 grams, supporting a better fat profile. Polyunsaturated fat is 0.3 grams, a weak mark for fruits.\n-\n14.7 grams of carbs. Sugar is 9 grams, supporting better carb quality. Starch is 0 grams, a meaningful miss for fruits.\n-\n1.1 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 1 out of 9, with 30% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin C is 78% daily value, useful for collagen formation and antioxidant support. Vitamin A is only 2% daily value, a weak mark for fruits.\n-\nPotassium is 6% daily value, useful for fluid balance. Iron is only 2% daily value, a weak mark for fruits.\n-\nPositives first: brings a clean fruit identity. It still contributes something valuable even when it is not elite. Easy to use as a whole food. Simple, recognisable, and realistic for everyday eating. Usually beats ultra-processed sweet snacks. That replacement value matters in real life.\n-\nDownsides next: can be easy to waste if timing is off. Perishability is a practical downside people actually feel. Protein contribution is tiny. That is normal for fruit, but it still limits nutritional breadth. Satiety is not especially strong. Fruit often needs help from the rest of the meal.\n-\nBig strengths are omega 3 and vitamin C, but the biggest weaknesses are easy to waste if timing is off and protein contribution is tiny.\n-\nA tier.\n"
