        "narrationText": "Skimmed Milk!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n0.1 grams of fat. Saturated fat is 0.1 grams, supporting a better fat profile. Omega 3 is 10 milligrams, a weak mark for dairy.\n-\n5 grams of carbs. Sugar is 5 grams, supporting better carb quality. Starch is 0 grams, a small miss for dairy.\n-\n3.4 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 7 out of 9, with 90% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance. Collagen is only 0 grams, a weak mark for dairy.\n-\nVitamin B12 is 20% daily value, useful for nerve and blood-cell support. Vitamin A is only 2% daily value, a weak mark for dairy.\n-\nCalcium is 10% daily value, adding useful mineral support. Magnesium is only 2% daily value, a weak mark for dairy.\n-\nPros first: easy protein delivery for normal diets. Dairy gets real credit for convenience. Easy to fit into familiar meals. That lowers friction for regular use. Slots into meals very easily. Practical versatility matters a lot for dairy.\n-\nDownsides next: processing changes the category a lot. The label alone does not tell the whole story. Fat quality varies wildly across dairy. Some dairy is clean, some really is not. Some versions get dense fast on fat, sodium, or both. The category swings a lot by format.\n-\nBig strengths are omega 3 and protein, but the biggest weaknesses are processing changes the category a lot and fat quality varies wildly across dairy.\n-\nB tier.\n"
