        "narrationText": "Orange!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n0.1 grams of fat. Saturated fat is 0 grams, supporting a better fat profile. Polyunsaturated fat is 0 grams, a weak mark for fruits.\n-\n11.8 grams of carbs. Glycemic index is 43 GI, supporting better carb quality. Starch is 0.2 grams, a meaningful miss for fruits.\n-\n0.9 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 1 out of 9, with 30% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin C is 59% daily value, useful for collagen formation and antioxidant support. Vitamin A is only 5% daily value, a weak mark for fruits.\n-\nPotassium is 4% daily value, useful for fluid balance. Iron is only 0% daily value, a weak mark for fruits.\n-\nPros first: vitamin C reputation is a real strength. That gives it a clear in-category edge. Brings a clean fruit identity. It still contributes something valuable even when it is not elite. Ingredient simplicity is hard to argue with. Whole-fruit clarity is part of the category appeal.\n-\nDownsides next: easy to eat quickly when it is very sweet. That can blur the line between light and sugar-heavy portions. Satiety is not especially strong. Fruit often needs help from the rest of the meal. Protein contribution is tiny. That is normal for fruit, but it still limits nutritional breadth.\n-\nBig strengths are omega 3 and carb quality, but the biggest weaknesses are easy to eat quickly when it is very sweet and satiety is not especially strong.\n-\nA tier.\n"
