        "narrationText": "Peanut Oil!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n100 grams of fat. Saturated fat is 17 grams, supporting a better fat profile. Omega 3 is 0 milligrams, and for oils and fats, that matters a lot.\n-\n0 grams of carbs. No defensible carb submacros to call out. Not relevant here.\n-\n0 grams of protein. No defensible protein submacros to call out. Also not relevant here.\n-\nVitamin E is 11% daily value, adding useful vitamin support. Vitamin A is only 0% daily value, a weak mark for oils and fats.\n-\nIron is 0% daily value, useful for oxygen transport. Magnesium is only 0% daily value, a weak mark for oils and fats.\n-\nPositives first: can help meals feel more complete and usable. Fats sometimes improve the whole plate, not just their own stats. Helps meals actually work. A fat that people can use well matters more than a theoretical one. Efficient energy source. That can be a strength in the right context.\n-\nDownsides next: small serving errors matter quickly. A little extra oil or fat can move the calorie math fast. Calorie density is brutally high. Tiny serving errors matter here. Easy to overpour without noticing. Practical portion creep is real.\n-\nBig strengths are fat quality and can help meals feel more complete and usable, but the biggest weaknesses are small serving errors matter quickly and calorie density is brutally high.\n-\nB tier.\n"
