        "narrationText": "Pomegranate!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n1.2 grams of fat. Saturated fat is 0.1 grams, supporting a better fat profile. Polyunsaturated fat is 0.1 grams, a weak mark for fruits.\n-\n18.7 grams of carbs. Glycemic index is 35 GI, supporting better carb quality. Starch is 0 grams, a meaningful miss for fruits.\n-\n1.7 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 1 out of 9, with 30% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin C is 12% daily value, useful for collagen formation and antioxidant support. Vitamin A is only 0% daily value, a weak mark for fruits.\n-\nPotassium is 5% daily value, useful for fluid balance. Iron is only 2% daily value, a weak mark for fruits.\n-\nPositives first: brings a clean fruit identity. It still contributes something valuable even when it is not elite. Ingredient simplicity is hard to argue with. Whole-fruit clarity is part of the category appeal. Usually beats ultra-processed sweet snacks. That replacement value matters in real life.\n-\nDownsides next: protein contribution is tiny. That is normal for fruit, but it still limits nutritional breadth. Easy to eat quickly when it is very sweet. That can blur the line between light and sugar-heavy portions. Sugar can climb fast. A lot of fruits look healthy but still lean sweet.\n-\nBig strengths are omega 3 and fibre, but the biggest weaknesses are protein contribution is tiny and easy to eat quickly when it is very sweet.\n-\nC tier.\n"
