        "narrationText": "Rye!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n1.6 grams of fat. Saturated fat is 0.2 grams, supporting a better fat profile. Omega 3 is 50 milligrams, a weak mark for grains.\n-\n75.9 grams of carbs. Fibre is 15.1 grams, helping with digestion and steadier meals.\n-\n10.3 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 4 out of 9, with 58% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin E is 1% daily value, adding useful vitamin support.\n-\nMagnesium is 31% daily value, adding useful mineral support. Potassium is 15% daily value, a weak mark for grains.\n-\nPositives first: can keep meals cheap and steady. That is a genuine category strength. Usually affordable and scalable. Practicality matters for staple foods. Easy to batch cook at scale. That practicality is part of why grains matter.\n-\nDownsides next: protein quality is limited. Even the better grains usually need pairing. Quality can drop fast with processing. The grain category punishes refinement pretty hard. Needs strong pairings to feel complete. The grain rarely finishes the nutrition job alone.\n-\nBig strengths are protein and fibre, but the biggest weaknesses are protein quality is limited and quality can drop fast with processing.\n-\nB tier.\n"
