        "narrationText": "Strawberries!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n0.3 grams of fat. Saturated fat is 0 grams, supporting a better fat profile. Polyunsaturated fat is 0.2 grams, a weak mark for fruits.\n-\n7.7 grams of carbs. Sugar is 4.9 grams, supporting better carb quality. Starch is 0 grams, a meaningful miss for fruits.\n-\n0.7 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 1 out of 9, with 30% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin C is 65% daily value, useful for collagen formation and antioxidant support. Vitamin A is only 0% daily value, a weak mark for fruits.\n-\nPotassium is 3% daily value, useful for fluid balance. Iron is only 2% daily value, a weak mark for fruits.\n-\nPositives first: sweetness is kept unusually well in check. That makes the fruit profile much cleaner than average. Usually beats ultra-processed sweet snacks. That replacement value matters in real life. Ingredient simplicity is hard to argue with. Whole-fruit clarity is part of the category appeal.\n-\nThe drawbacks next: protein contribution is tiny. That is normal for fruit, but it still limits nutritional breadth. Can be easy to waste if timing is off. Perishability is a practical downside people actually feel. Satiety is not especially strong. Fruit often needs help from the rest of the meal.\n-\nBig strengths are omega 3 and carb quality, but the biggest weaknesses are protein contribution is tiny and easy to waste if timing is off.\n-\nS tier.\n"
