        "narrationText": "Tuna!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n0.8 grams of fat. Saturated fat is 0.2 grams, supporting a better fat profile. Polyunsaturated fat is 0.2 grams, and for meats, fat quality is a major tradeoff.\n-\n0 grams of carbs. No defensible carb submacros to call out. In this category, carbs barely matter.\n-\n28 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 9 out of 9, with 95% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance. Collagen is only 0.3 grams, so for meats, it is not bringing much connective-tissue protein.\n-\nVitamin B12 is 43% daily value, useful for nerve and blood-cell support. Vitamin D is 9% daily value, a small miss for meats.\n-\nPotassium is 9% daily value, useful for fluid balance. Zinc is only 5% daily value, a weak mark for meats.\n-\nThe upsides first: complete protein delivery is reliable. The category is supposed to do this well. Can control hunger well in real meals. That is a major reason the category stays relevant. Gives a straightforward protein solution. The category often wins on simplicity and reliability.\n-\nCons next: cost and storage can be a pain. Practical downsides still count. Sourcing quality can change the real-world result a lot. Not every version of the same meat is equal. The outcome depends heavily on preparation. A good raw profile can still be cooked into a worse result.\n-\nBig strengths are omega 3 and protein, but the biggest weaknesses are cost and storage can be a pain and sourcing quality can change the real-world result a lot.\n-\nA tier.\n"
