        "narrationText": "Yam!\n-\nRanked!\n-\n0.2 grams of fat. Omega 3 is 20 milligrams, supporting a more useful fat profile. Polyunsaturated fat is 0.1 grams, a weak mark for tubers.\n-\n27.9 grams of carbs. Fibre is 4.1 grams, helping with digestion and steadier meals.\n-\n1.5 grams of protein. Essential amino acids score is 2 out of 9, with 45% bioavailability, making the protein useful for repair and maintenance.\n-\nVitamin C is 29% daily value, useful for collagen formation and antioxidant support. Vitamin A is 11% daily value, a weak mark for tubers.\n-\nPotassium is 19% daily value, useful for fluid balance. Iron is only 4% daily value, a weak mark for tubers.\n-\nPositives first: practical staple carb source. Tubers are often judged by how usable they are day to day. Can be quite filling. A useful practical strength when the preparation stays sensible. Whole-food carb package. That matters because it is a cleaner baseline than heavily refined carbs.\n-\nDownsides next: satiety payoff depends a lot on format. Some versions are much steadier than others. Still a carb-heavy category. That is the core ceiling tubers have to manage. Can go downhill fast when heavily processed. The category has a big preparation trap.\n-\nBig strengths are omega 3 and protein, but the biggest weaknesses are satiety payoff depends a lot on format and still a carb-heavy category.\n-\nS tier.\n"
